WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.060
Terraform helper scripts often run in CI/CD, which means pipeline controls become part of Terraform security.

2
00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:20.120
Branch filters, protected tags, protected environments, manual approvals, runner permissions, secret scope, backend access, and artifact retention all influence what the helper can do.

3
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:28.220
A script that is safe on a trusted workstation may need additional controls when it runs automatically in response to repository events.

4
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.060
Logs should provide operational evidence without exposing sensitive data.

5
00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:43.080
Useful records include the automation identity, repository, commit or release reference, workspace, backend category, environment, high-level action, and result.

6
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:51.500
Logs should avoid raw secrets, provider credentials, sensitive state, unnecessary plan details, and excessive output dumps.

7
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.720
Good logging helps teams investigate without creating another place where sensitive infrastructure information spreads.

8
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:12.520
Monitoring should cover failed runs, repeated lock failures, unexpected drift, unusual identities, unexpected resource changes, policy exceptions, and jobs that run outside expected branch or environment boundaries.

9
00:01:12.620 --> 00:01:23.260
Drift is not automatically malicious, but it can reveal manual changes, broken workflows, missed approvals, or configuration that no longer matches the reviewed source.

10
00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:29.840
Monitoring gives teams an opportunity to respond before small differences become larger operational problems.

11
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:34.920
Recovery planning matters because not every infrastructure change can be automatically undone.

12
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:49.180
Before high-impact workflows are automated, teams should understand how they would pause a pipeline, revoke credentials, review state, restore a known-good configuration, re-run a validated plan, or coordinate a manual recovery process.

13
00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:59.920
Incidents and near misses should feed back into stronger guardrails: clearer validation, narrower credentials, better approvals, improved logging, and better operational review.
